    type: "MCQ",
    marksCorrect: 4,
    marksWrong: -1,
    questionText: `A chemistry olympiad-style prompt states: Two colourless solutions have $pH=1$ and $pH=13$. If equal volumes are mixed and the reaction is not assumed to be a simple pH average, what must be determined first to predict the final pH?`,
    isPyq: false,
    options: [
      { id: "A", text: `only the average of the two pH values` },
      { id: "B", text: `the reacting amounts of acid/base species and their concentrations` },
      { id: "C", text: `only the density of water` },
      { id: "D", text: `only the colours of the original solutions` },
    ],
    correct: "B",
    explanation: [
      { title: `Reject averaging`, content: `pH is logarithmic, so directly averaging two pH values generally has no chemical basis.` },
      { title: `Convert concept`, content: `One must relate pH to hydrogen/hydroxide concentration and then account for neutralisation.` },
      { title: `Compare capacities`, content: `The reacting amounts determine which species remains in excess.` },
      { title: `Then calculate`, content: `Only after stoichiometry is settled can the final concentration and pH be found.` },
      { title: `Conclude`, content: `The first required step is determining the reacting acid/base amounts and concentrations.` },
    ],
    concepts: ["pH reasoning", "neutralisation", "logarithmic scale"],
